  "hints": [
    "Start by handing every child exactly one candy, then ask which neighbour constraints are still broken.",
    "Each child is constrained only by the child to its left and the child to its right, so try to satisfy one side completely before touching the other.",
    "Sweep left to right for rising ratings, sweep right to left for falling ratings keeping the larger value, and finally add up the piles."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force gives every child one candy and then repeatedly sweeps the line, raising any child that currently ties or loses against a lower rated neighbour, until a whole sweep makes no change. Each sweep is O(n) and up to n sweeps are needed, because a run of increasing ratings can only grow one step per sweep, giving O(n^2). The optimal solution notices that the constraint graph is a path, so two sweeps suffice: left to right to satisfy every greater-than-the-left-neighbour requirement, then right to left to satisfy the rest, keeping the larger of the pile already assigned and the pile the right neighbour forces.",
    "why_optimal": "The minimum valid assignment is the pointwise smallest vector obeying all neighbour constraints, and the two sweeps compute exactly that vector: the first pass fixes every child whose rating rises to the right, the second fixes every falling run, and no third pass is ever needed because a child is constrained only by its two immediate neighbours. Along a strictly increasing run of length L the last child needs at least L candies, so no algorithm returns a smaller total, and both sweeps read the ratings a constant number of times, which makes O(n) optimal. The bound of 200 children keeps the O(n^2) version fast, but the two passes are the intended answer.",
    "pitfalls": "Running only the left-to-right sweep and leaving descending runs broken: ratings [3, 2, 1] needs the piles [3, 2, 1], not [1, 1, 1]. Overwriting the pile in the second sweep instead of taking the maximum, which breaks local peaks such as [1, 3, 2, 1]. Treating equal ratings as a strict inequality, which inflates the count on flat stretches. Initialising the piles at zero instead of one, and forgetting that a single child returns 1."
  },
